cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its weights, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "Platonic-Engram-HNet v2: Disaggregating Compute and Memory Below 1 MB — Tokenizer-Free int8 Byte Routing, O(1) Flash Engrams, Causal Chrono-CfC and Frame-Locked Decoding in Bit-Exact C/C++20 ESP32-S3 Firmware"
version: "2.0.0"
date-released: "2026-11-08"
license: MIT
authors:
  - family-names: Zhou
    given-names: Robert
    orcid: https://orcid.org/0009-0009-2155-7215
    affiliation: Colby College
    website: https://robertzhou.dev
url: https://robertzhou.dev/work/platonic-engram-hnet/
repository-code: https://github.com/bobzhou-source/platonic-engram-hnet
abstract: >-
  A sub-1 MB on-device language stack for an ESP32-S3 e-reader that splits the
  problem along the memory hierarchy instead of shrinking a transformer: a
  95 KB looped int8 byte router in PSRAM (no tokenizer), a 4.2 MB O(1) Engram
  hash table on SD read one 64-byte slot per word, four continuous-time
  Chrono-CfC entity slots behind causal spoiler gates, and a frame-locked
  concept decoder over a 4,096-clause bank. PyTorch QAT, NumPy, C11 and the
  C++20 firmware kernel agree bit for bit.
keywords:
  - embedded-ai
  - esp32-s3
  - int8
  - quantization
  - byte-level-lm
  - engram
  - e-ink
  - c-plus-plus
  - edge-ml
  - tokenizer-free
